By the year 2150, humanity had already reached the stars, but no one expected Earth’s geese to follow. It all started with a research experiment—scientists launched a few geese into space aboard a satellite, intending to study their behavior in microgravity. To everyone's astonishment, the geese not only survived but thrived. Their wings grew stronger, their feathers adapted to the harsh conditions of space, and soon they began flying in patterns that defied explanation.
Over time, a new breed of space-faring geese emerged. Their once seasonal migrations turned into interstellar journeys, and a special flock known as the Starfeathers led the way. The leader of this flock was a wise old gander named Orion, whose keen instincts guided them toward unexplored realms of space.
Orion’s flock, with their cosmic feathers glowing in the distant starlight, became the first migratory species to explore beyond the solar system. They navigated through asteroid fields, drifted past nebulae, and glided around planets, honking in harmony as they flew across the void.
But their most incredible adventure came when they encountered a black hole. Its powerful pull threatened to consume everything in its path. Yet, the geese did not fear. They formed a spiraling formation, using their wings to harness the space winds and create a vortex of energy that defied the black hole’s pull.
In an incredible feat of coordination, they emerged safely on the other side—into a new, unexplored region of the universe. The Starfeathers had transcended the limits of Earth’s sky and become explorers of the cosmos, their honks echoing across galaxies, guiding the way for future space travelers.